@database "http_server"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: http_server.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "HTTP Server"
@{h1}HTTP Server

The HTTP server puts one plugin behind a small REST interface, so that a
program outside ScarabHive -- a script, a cron job, another service -- can
call that plugin's tools with a plain HTTP request. You start it from the
command line, name the plugin to serve, and it answers on
@{tt}http://127.0.0.1:9000@{utt} until you stop it. It is not a web server
for files, and it makes no outgoing requests of its own: every
@{tt}/call@{utt} runs a tool of the plugin it serves.

Whoever can reach @{tt}/call@{utt} can run every tool of that plugin. Read
@{" Who can call it " link security} before you serve a plugin that writes
files or runs commands.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Starting it  " link cli}
  @{"  The HTTP interface  " link http}
  @{"  Who can call it  " link security}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node cli "Starting it"
@{h1}Starting it

From the repository root, with the virtual environment's Python:

@{code}
python -m plugins.http_server --server-name datetime
@{body}

It prints the two URLs and serves until Ctrl+C. The process belongs to
you, not to a chat session or the API: it runs until it is stopped, and
stopping the API does not stop it.

@{h2}Options

@{table}
Option | Default | Meaning
--server-name | required | the plugin to serve: an instance name from @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}, or a plugin type
--host | 127.0.0.1 | the address to bind; anything but a loopback address needs an API key
--port | 9000 | the port to listen on
--config | AGENT_CONFIG_PATH, else config/config.yaml | the main config file to read the plugin's entry and the system settings from
--no-ssl-verify | off | sets @{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt} to false, for plugins that read that setting
--version | -- | prints the version and exits
@{body}

@{h2}Which plugin, with which settings

The name is looked up in @{tt}plugins.servers@{utt} of the loaded
configuration first. When an entry has that name, the plugin is built from
it -- its type, its settings -- the way the API builds it; whether the entry
is enabled does not matter. Otherwise the name is taken as a plugin type
and the plugin gets no settings of its own. An unknown name ends the
program with the list of plugin types it knows.

The served plugin's @{tt}start_plugin@{utt} hook runs before the server
takes requests, its @{tt}stop_plugin@{utt} hook after it stops. It is a
second instance, in a process of its own: it shares nothing held in memory
with the API's instance of the same plugin -- only what the plugin keeps on
disk. Plugins that need a running agent (a sub-agent, a session service)
do not work here.

@{h2}When it does not start

@{bullet}A non-loopback @{tt}--host@{utt} without a key: "Error running
HTTP server: http_server refuses to bind non-loopback host '0.0.0.0'
without authentication: ...", exit code 1.
@{bullet}The port is taken: the bind error is logged ("error while
attempting to bind on address ..."), exit code 1.
@{bullet}The plugin cannot be built: "Error creating server '<name>':
<reason>", exit code 1.

The log goes to the console and to @{tt}logs/http_server.log@{utt},
relative to the directory you started it in.
@endnode

@node http "The HTTP interface"
@{h1}The HTTP interface

@{h2}GET /health

Always open, no key needed. Answers
@{tt}{"status": "ok", "server": "<name>"}@{utt} with the name the plugin
was started under.

@{h2}POST /call

Runs one tool of the served plugin. The body is JSON:

@{table}
Field | Meaning
tool | the tool's name, e.g. @{tt}datetime_operations@{utt}; required
params | the tool's arguments as an object; default @{tt}{}@{utt}
@{body}

@{code}
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9000/call -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"tool": "datetime_operations", "params": {"operation": "current"}}'
@{body}

The answer is the tool's result, as the tool returned it. The tool is
called as the API calls it: with a status scope, so plugins that report
progress work, and through @{tt}call()@{utt} for plugins that have no
status support. Parameters whose name starts with @{tt}_@{utt} are dropped
before the call (see @{" Who can call it " link security}).

@{h2}Answers that are not the tool's

@{table}
Status | Body | When
200 | @{tt}{"error": "Call failed: <message>", "tool": ..., "params": ...}@{utt} | the tool raised; @{tt}params@{utt} is what was sent
401 | @{tt}{"detail": "Unauthorized: missing or invalid API key"}@{utt} | a key is set and the request has none or a wrong one
403 | @{tt}{"detail": "Forbidden: host is not loopback"}@{utt} | no key is set and the request's Host header is not a loopback name
403 | @{tt}{"detail": "Forbidden: origin is not loopback"}@{utt} | no key is set and the request has an Origin header that is not a loopback URL
415 | @{tt}{"detail": "Content-Type must be application/json"}@{utt} | any other content type, or none -- with or without a key
422 | FastAPI's validation error | no @{tt}tool@{utt}, or a body that is not a JSON object
@{body}

An unknown tool is a raised error, so it answers 200 with, for example,
"Call failed: Tool 'nope' not found in datetime. Available tools:
['datetime_operations']. Expected method: nope()". A tool that returns an
error of its own (@{tt}{"error": ...}@{utt}) is answered as it is, with
status 200. The adapter sets no timeout: a request waits as long as the
tool runs.
@endnode

@node security "Who can call it"
@{h1}Who can call it

@{tt}/call@{utt} runs any tool of the served plugin with that plugin's full
power -- the served terminal runs commands, a file plugin writes files. No
agent allowlist, role or approval hook sits in between: those belong to
agents, and there is no agent here.

@{h2}Without a key

Without a key the server binds only a loopback address (127.0.0.1,
localhost, ::1); it refuses to start on any other. @{tt}/call@{utt} then
answers only requests whose Host header names a loopback host, and whose
Origin header, when there is one, does too; it refuses the others with
403. That shuts out a web page in your browser that reaches 127.0.0.1
through DNS rebinding, or that posts to it from another site. And
@{tt}/call@{utt} takes only @{tt}Content-Type: application/json@{utt}
(else 415): a web page can send that to another site only after a
preflight, which the server does not answer.

What it does not keep out: every program and every user on the same
machine. On a shared host, set a key.

@{h2}With a key

Set the key in the environment variable
@{tt}HTTP_SERVER_AUTH_KEY@{utt} before you start it. @{tt}/call@{utt} then
needs @{tt}X-API-Key: <key>@{utt} or
@{tt}Authorization: Bearer <key>@{utt}, compared in constant time. With a key, any host may be bound and any Host header
is accepted. The server speaks plain HTTP: on a network, the key and every
answer travel unencrypted unless a TLS proxy stands in front of it.

@{h2}Users and sessions

The server knows no users: everyone with the key -- or, without one,
everyone on the machine -- is the same caller. Plugins that keep data per
user or per session learn who is calling from parameters the agent runtime
sets (@{tt}_user_id@{utt}, @{tt}_session_id@{utt} and others starting with
@{tt}_@{utt}). The server drops every parameter starting with
@{tt}_@{utt} from a call, so a caller cannot claim to be a user or a
session. Such plugins then act as they do when called outside any session.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Nothing. The plugin gives agents no tool, adds nothing to the system
prompt and sends no status line, enabled or not. It is a command-line
program: inside the API or agent-cli nobody starts its HTTP interface or
hands it a plugin to serve, so a tool could only fail there -- and one
that worked would let an agent run another plugin's tools past its own
allowlist.

The plugin served over HTTP is not touched either: agents see its tools
as they always do, and a call over @{tt}/call@{utt} never reaches a chat.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Without a key, every local program and user can call the served
plugin.
@{bullet}Plain HTTP only; no TLS.
@{bullet}No timeout on @{tt}/call@{utt}.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing needs to be enabled: the command line takes the plugin to serve
from the configuration and its own settings from its options. The entry in
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} has no effect on it, and enabling it gives
agents nothing:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    http_server:
      type: http_server
      enabled: false
      host: 127.0.0.1
      port: 9000
@{body}

It needs FastAPI and uvicorn, which the core already has. It keeps no data
of its own.

@{h2}Settings

@{table}
Setting | Where from | Default | Meaning
host | --host | 127.0.0.1 | the address to bind
port | --port | 9000 | the port
API key | the environment's HTTP_SERVER_AUTH_KEY | none | required on /call when set
@{body}

The @{tt}config:@{utt} block in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} only
documents host and port; the defaults above are what the code applies.
@endnode
